## Modular $\b_{0_{2}}$ Electroreduction Activity in Triphenylene-Based Metal-Organic Frameworks
DOI: 10.1039/x0xx00000x www.rsc.org/
Triphenylene ligands hexasubstituted with amino or phenol groups afford two phases of electrically conductive layered twodimensional metal-organic frameworks upon reaction with various metals. Regardless of the identity of the metal or chelating atom, $\pmb{\pi}$ −stacking within the MOF layers is essential to achieving high electrical conductivity, redox activity, and catalytic activity.
Control over the architectural and electronic properties of heterogeneous catalysts poses a major obstacle in the targeted design of active, stable, and economically sustainable materials for producing fuels.1 Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are compelling choices for electrocatalytic applications as their high surface area and tunable porosity and ligand structure affords densely packed active sites and tailor-made microenvironments for controllable reaction conditions within the pores. Despite the high potential for MOF-based electrocatalysts, synthesis of these materials often involves chelation of hard metal ions to hard N or O atoms in redoxinactive ligands. Typical compositions thus offer no low energy charge transport pathways or charge carriers, rendering these materials electrically insulating.2 Excitingly, the emergence of intrinsically conducting or semiconducting metal-organic species2–9 has made accessible the use of such materials as tunable, high surface area electrocatalysts for energy conversion reactions such as H2 evolution,10,11 $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ evolution,11,12 $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction,8 $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ reduction,13,1 4 and others.15 The high level of atomic definition in these materials offers an opportunity to gain insight into the operative catalytic mechanisms and establish structure-function behavior. In particular, mechanistic studies of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathsf{H}\mathsf{I}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P}=}\end{array}$ 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene) revealed that the $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ reduction reaction (ORR) proceeds on a ligand-based active site, and suggested that the metal identity and electron delocalization throughout the framework could have important implications for the electronic structure which in turn should govern electrocatalytic activity.16 Herein, we probe the role of the metal identity, chelating atom, and $\pi$ -stacking in a suite of triphenylene-based conductive MOFs in influencing the ORR activity and mechanism, with the goal of identifying the key players in this class for MOFs for dictating electrocatalytic behavior.
To this end, we synthesized several analogues featuring a hexasubstituted triphenylene core. As shown in Fig. 1, chelation of a divalent first-row transition metal with either the hexaamino or hexahydroxytriphenylene (HHTP) ligand in the presence of base and air affords one of two phases. The three analogues of the hexagonal crystal system, $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2},$ $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2},$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ (referred to as the hexagonal MOFs), feature a 2D honeycomb lattice stacked in a slipped parallel configuration along the c axis (Fig. 1a and c).3,4,7 The two analogues of the trigonal crystal system, ${\mathsf C}_{0_{3}}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ (referred to as the trigonal MOFs), feature alternating layers of the honeycomb lattice and trinuclear $\mathsf{M}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{12}$ clusters that are rotated $60^{\circ}$ with respect to the honeycomb lattice (Fig. 1b and d).7  Isolation of the hexagonal MOFs as well as the trigonal MOFs was confirmed with powder X-ray diffraction (Fig. 1c and d).
The ORR activity of the MOF powders deposited on glassy carbon electrodes was probed in ${\mathsf{p H}}13$ and 8. The reductive current shown in Fig. 2 is observed for all analogues only under $\begin{array}{r}{\mathsf{O}_{2},}\end{array}$ confirming that the current indeed stems from $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ reduction (Fig. $\mathsf{S1}^{\dagger}\big)$ . The cyclic voltammograms under $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ atmosphere shown in Fig. 2 reveal that the hexagonal MOF ${\mathsf{C u}}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ exhibits the highest initial ORR activity in oth pH environments. However, the instability of this analogue to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ under experimental conditions resulted in a rapid loss of acGvity aHer the first CV cycle (Fig. S2†). This instability prevented collection of reliable data for ORR with $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2},$ as the catalyst activity continuously declined during prolonged data collection. The other hexagonal MOFs, $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2},$ reduce $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ with lower overpotential and higher current density than the trigonal MOFs. Although the trigonal MOFs ${\mathsf C}\circ_{3}({\mathsf H}{\mathsf H}{\mathsf T}{\mathsf P})_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ seem to contribute no catalytic activity beyond the glassy carbon background current, depositing these analogues on the more inert indium tin oxide showed nominal ORR activity (Fig. ${\mathsf{S}}3^{\dagger}{\boldsymbol{\mathit{I}}}$ . This substrate
independent activity confirmed that kinetic data collected for these analogues on glassy carbon rotating disk electrodes represents the ORR kinetics of the MOFs rather than simply the blank electrodes. Potentiostatic reduction of $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ over 8 hours in ${\mathsf{p H}}\ 13$ revealed that the HHTP-based MOFs deactivate more quickly in base than does $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ (Table $\mathsf{S1^{\dagger}})$ . This is likely due to thermodynamic differences arising from better energetic and / or spatial overlap between the HITP ligand and Ni orbitals than the HHTP ligand and metal orbitals. The resulting longer metal-ligand bond distances in the HHTP MOFs could enable more facile decomposition of these analogues compared to that which features Ni-N coordination.7,17–19
To determine the product distribution of ORR with the triphenylene MOFs and how this may change as a function of increasing driving force, potentiostatic rotating ring disk electrode (RRDE) studies were conducted over the ORR potential window (Fig. ${\mathsf{S}}4^{\dagger}.$ ). Dividing the anodic current passed at the Pt ring from backoxidizing the $2e^{-}$ reduction product by the total cathodic current passed at the MOF-modified disk from both the $2e^{-}$ and $4\mathsf{e}^{-}$ reduction products (hydrogen peroxide and water, respectively) gives the potential-dependent faradaic efficiency plots shown in Fig. ${\sf S}{\sf5}^{\sf+}$ . Unsurprisingly,20 all MOFs exhibit potential-dependent faradaic efficiency, with the $4\mathsf{e}^{-}$ reduction product favored when more than ${\sim}300\mathrm{mV}$ of overpotential is applied, with the $2e^{-}$ reduction product favored at lower overpotentials.
The kinetic rate laws for ORR with the hexagonal and trigonal MOFs were obtained by probing the order in $[0_{2}],~[{\mathsf{H}}^{+}],$ and electrons. Activation-controlled Tafel plots (Fig. 3) generated by Koutecky-Levich data collected in ${\mathsf{p H}}\ 8$ and 13 (Fig. S6†) reveal slopes of $110{-}170~\mathsf{m V}{\cdot}\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ (Table 1). A slope of $118\mathrm{\mV\cdotdec}^{-1}$ is indicative of rate-limiting electron transfer,21 which we believe to be relevant for all analogues here. One notable exception is the Tafel data collected for ${\mathsf C}\circ_{3}({\mathsf H}{\mathsf H}{\mathsf T}{\mathsf P})_{2}$ in ${\mathsf{p H}}13$ , which features a Tafel slope of $81\mathrm{~mV}\cdot\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ . This slope could indicate that in ${\mathsf{p H}}\ 13$ , ORR with ${\mathsf C}\circ_{3}({\mathsf H}{\mathsf H}{\mathsf T}{\mathsf P})_{2}$ does not proceed with rate-limiting electron transfer, but rather perhaps with rate-limiting O2 chemisorption.22 This pH-dependent electrokinetic behavior is consistent with the behavior Co-macrocycles active for ORR catalysis.23 The Tafel data not only gives insight into whether rate-limiting electron transfer is relevant, but also provides relative rate constants as a means to directly compare catalytic activity (Table ${\mathsf{S}}2^{\dagger}$ ). The placement of the Tafel slopes from $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ in the lower right quadrant of the Tafel plot (Fig. 3, dashed triangle) reflects the fast electrokinetics of this analogue at both ${\mathsf{p H}}8$ and 13, i.e. $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ passes a high cathodic current density (log(j)) with low overpotential (η).21,22
Potentiostatic data collected with varying overpotential and varying concentrations of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ in the electrolyte shows that for all analogues, more cathodic current (I) can be passed with increasing $[{\mathsf{O}}_{2}]$ in $\mathsf{p H}8$ (Fig. ${\mathsf{S7}}^{\dagger}$ ). This linear increase in $\log(1)$ versus $\log([0_{2}])$ with slopes of ${\sim}1$ (Table ${\mathsf{S}}3^{\dagger}.$ ) over the ORR potential window indicates that all analogues follow first-order kinetics in $[{\mathsf O}_{2}]$ . Experimental and computational data previously reported for ORR with $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ suggested rate-limiting $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ binding concomitant with $\tt e^{-}$ transfer to $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ to form the superoxide adduct was likely.13,16 The similar $[{\mathsf{O}}_{2}]$ order and Tafel data of the triphenylene MOF analogues (with the exception of ORR with ${\mathsf C}\circ_{3}({\mathsf H}{\mathsf H}{\mathsf T}{\mathsf P})_{2}$ in pH 13) supports the assertion that all other triphenylene MOFs reported here also exhibit rate-limiting electron transfer- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}{\bf{0}}_{2}$ binding to the catalyst during ORR.
Given that the Tafel data from all analogues points to ORR proceeding with rate-limiting electron transfer, and considering the protonated nature of the ORR products, we investigated the possibility of proton-coupled electron transfer (PCET) occurring in our systems. Galvanostatic data from each analogue was collected under $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ while titrating the electrolyte from ${\mathsf{p H}}13.5$ to ${\mathsf{p H}}8$ (Fig. 4). As shown in Table 2, all hexagonal MOFs exhibit a non-zero dependence of the ORR overpotential with $[\mathsf{H}^{+}],$ whereas the trigonal MOFs feature $\delta\mathsf{E}/\delta\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}$ slopes of nearly zero. The independence of ORR overpotential on $[\mathsf{H}^{\dagger}]$ during ORR with the trigonal MOFs suggests that no electron transfer during ORR with those analogues is proton-coupled.24 In contrast, all hexagonal MOFs exhibit a non-zero order in $[\mathsf{H}^{\dagger}]$ . As seen with $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2},^{16}$ the δE/δpH slopes for all hexagonal $M O F s^{\ddagger}$ are ${\sim}20~\mathsf{m V}{\cdot}\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ , revealing a fractional $[\mathsf{H}^{\dagger}]$ dependence that rules out rate-limiting PCET.24
To explore the relationship between electron transfer and available proton concentration, cyclic voltammetry was run on the various MOFs under an inert atmosphere in varying pH environments. As previously observed with $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2},^{16}$ the hexagonal analogues $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ undergo oxidation events that exhibit Nernstian pH dependence, i.e. are protoncoupled (Fig. S8 and ${\sf S9}^{\dagger}$ ).24 As with $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H}\mathsf{I}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{P})_{2},$ the fractional $[\mathsf{H}^{\dagger}]$ order observed in ORR with the hexagonal analogues likely stems from PCET in the MOF-hydroperoxide adduct. If the rate of this second, proton-coupled electron transfer to $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ is only marginally faster than the rate-limiting first electron transfer to $\phantom{+}0_{2},$ some fractional $[\mathsf{H}^{\dagger}]$ may experimentally manifest. Fractional order in $[\mathsf{H}^{\dagger}]$ during ORR has also been observed in ORR with nitrogen-doped graphitic carbon25 and precious metal catalysts.26,27
